# HyperMarkDown

**HyperMarkDown** (`.hmd`) is this project's most generic and most-used
knowledge format: ordinary Markdown, plus rich visuals, plus wiki links into a
knowledge graph. A **card** is one `.hmd` file that explains one idea and whose
value is in what it links to; every file under `doc/wiki/` is a card. The
baseline is CommonMark 0.31.2 taken whole, so a `.hmd` file reads fine as plain
Markdown and any `.md` file is already syntactically valid `.hmd`.

This card owns the **syntax**. It follows the upstream
[HyperMarkDown documentation](https://hypermarkdown.org/); the
[tutorial](https://hypermarkdown.org/wiki/hmd-tutorial/) introduces the language
and the [language specification](https://hypermarkdown.org/wiki/hmd-lang-spec/)
is the normative reference.

## Three layers

The format stacks three layers, each preserving the one below:

- **CommonMark** — headings, paragraphs, emphasis, lists, blockquotes, fenced
  code, inline links. Unchanged.
- **Rich layer** — tables, task lists, strikethrough, footnotes, callouts, TeX
  mathematics, D2 diagrams. Assumed present, not optional extensions.
- **Hyper layer** — the constructs below, which turn a file into a node in a
  graph.

## The six hyper constructs

| Written | Means |
| --- | --- |
| `[[card]]` | link to a card |
| `[[card\|words]]` | link, showing different words |
| `[[card#Heading]]` | link to a section of a card |
| `text ^name` | give this block a name |
| `[[card#^name]]` | link to a named block |
| `![[…]]` | embed any of the above instead of linking |

A **wikilink** names its target by filename without the `.hmd` extension. The
display text after `|` lets the sentence read naturally. A `#` addresses a
heading inside the target and is forgiving about capitalization and spacing,
since it matches against the heading's slug.

A **block anchor** is a trailing `^name` at the end of a block's last line,
preceded by at least one space and followed only by the line ending. The anchor
is not part of the block's content and is not rendered. Anchor identifiers are
scoped to one card and must be unique within it.

```markdown
A token is valid for exactly one rotation window. ^definition

See [[hmd-tutorial#^definition]] and [[md-hmd-interop|the interop notes]].
```

An **embed** is any wikilink with a `!` in front: the content is pulled in where
you wrote it rather than linked. Embedding a whole card expands its body with
the frontmatter removed; embedding a heading expands that heading through the
line before the next heading of the same or higher level; embedding a block
expands the anchored block with the anchor stripped. Expansion is recursive,
stops at sixteen levels, and a cycle is an error rather than a hang.

```markdown
![[glossary/token]]
![[glossary/token#Rotation]]
![[glossary/token#^definition]]
```

## Frontmatter

A card may open with a YAML block bounded by `---`. Four keys are reserved;
authors may add their own.

```yaml
---
tags: [area/auth, status/accepted]
use: [no_autodiscovery]
import:
  - from /shared import tokens as shared-tokens
  - from /glossary import *
nav:
  order: 10
  visibility: public
---
```

- **`tags`** say what a card is *about*, hierarchically with `/`. Folders say
  where it *lives*. Tags are not link targets.
- **`use`** toggles features; an `no_` prefix turns one off. It **inherits** —
  placed in a folder's `index.hmd` it governs that whole subtree.
- **`import`** binds names explicitly. `import x as y` takes priority over the
  spine; `import *` adds a search origin that runs after the spine and before
  autodiscovery. Imports do **not** inherit.
- **`nav`** carries publication metadata. `order` is an ascending integer;
  `visibility` is `public` or `private` and decides whether the card is
  published at all. Publication is opt-in — the default is private.

Frontmatter is optional, and no card under `doc/wiki/` currently uses it.

## Name resolution

The **namespace root** is `doc/wiki/`. That is the upstream default for the
`wiki` setting in `.hmd/config.toml`, and this repository has no such config, so
the default stands. The graph is therefore closed to `doc/wiki/`: a `[[ ]]` link
resolves only to a card in the wiki space, and a reference that escapes the root
is an error. Anything outside — `src/`, proposals, model specs — is reached with
an ordinary relative Markdown link.

A reference takes one of four forms:

| Form | Kind |
| --- | --- |
| `[[login]]` | bare name — one segment |
| `[[specs/login]]` | unqualified path — several segments |
| `[[./login]]`, `[[../login]]` | relative to this card |
| `[[/specs/login]]` | absolute from the namespace root |

An unqualified reference is tried in four phases, and the first match stops
resolution:

1. a **named import** — bare names only;
2. the **spine** — this card's own directory, then its parent, outward to the
   root, each probed non-recursively. The spine is a straight line up and never
   searches sideways, so adding a card elsewhere cannot silently change what a
   link means;
3. **wildcard import origins**, in declaration order;
4. **autodiscovery** — a sweep of the whole tree. It does look sideways, and it
   requires a unique match; two candidates are an error, not a coin flip.

A directory's `index.hmd` is its landing page, and `[[glossary]]` and
`[[glossary/index]]` are two spellings of the same card.

## The rich layer

```markdown
| Construct | Written |
| --- | --- |

- [x] a task list item
- [ ] an unfinished one

Strikethrough is ~~two tildes either side~~. Footnotes[^1] attach a marker.

[^1]: The note itself, which may sit anywhere in the file.

!!! note "A callout takes a kind and a title"
    Its body is indented four spaces.

??? tip "Opened with ??? instead, a callout starts collapsed"

Inline maths is $n$, and display maths stands alone:

$$ t_n = U\bigl(0,\; \min(t_{max},\; b \cdot 2^n)\bigr) $$
```

Diagrams are D2 in a fenced block tagged `d2`:

````markdown
```d2
direction: right
markdown -> HyperMarkDown: adds a graph
```
````

## Reserved characters

Inside a link target the characters `[`, `]`, `|`, `#`, `^`, and newline are
reserved, and **there is no escape mechanism**. To show any hyper construct
literally — as this card does throughout — put it in a code span or a fenced
block.

## Checking the graph

The upstream `hmd` CLI parses, resolves, and reports: `hmd lint` is the primary
command, `hmd info` prints the resolved root and discovery policy, `hmd graph`
dumps the resolved graph, and `hmd render` expands embeds and rewrites links.

A link to a card that does not exist is a **warning** (`HMD001`) — **forward
links are deliberate**, marking a concept worth writing later. Errors are
reserved for links that cannot mean one thing: an ambiguous autodiscovery match
(`HMD002`), a duplicate block anchor in one card (`HMD006`), and malformed
wikilink syntax (`HMD010`).

## Writing a card

A card explains one concept and nothing else. Cross-reference other cards
liberally so a whole model emerges from small, connected cards rather than one
long document, and keep it **DRY and bidirectional**: never restate what another
card owns — link to it — and make each idea reachable from both directions.

The authoring procedure — naming, structure, and register — is owned by the
`doc/hmd` documentation style, not restated here:
[doc/hmd](../../.grem/styles/doc/hmd/prompt.md). Running
`grem new --type doc --style hmd <source>` renders that style against a source
document for an agent to apply.

## Where it is used

Every card in `doc/wiki/` is hyper-markdown, including the [[kanban]] board
convention. It is one of the notations the project's design layer under
`doc/models/` may be written in, alongside more formal specifications.
